; Decoration: a sprite and nothing else, exactly as in Unity - no collider, no body. The backdrop, the
; moon, the distant arches, the ground rim, the step rims and the arena gates are all this scene.
; Replaces the node GameplayEnvironmentBuilder.CreateSceneryPiece built with GameplayBuildShim.NewObject.
;
; A one-node scene looks like a file for no structure, and the builder's header used to say so. Decision
; D8 rejected that: the argument applies to any prefab small enough, and rule 2 is about where a
; reusable thing LIVES, not how many nodes it has. A piece of scenery is instanced six times from one
; place, which makes it a prefab whatever its node count.
;
; NO TEXTURE, and there cannot be one: GameplayVisualFactory.CreateSquareSprite generates the image at
; runtime, so there is no baked Resources/Art file for an ext_resource to point at. Same reason
; Scenes/World/SolidBox.tscn authors none.
;
; WHAT IS BOUND PER INSTANCE, all of it designer-owned: the name and the position (SceneLayout_*.json
; via GameplaySceneDefaults, position written BEFORE the instance enters the tree), the texture, the
; size and the pivot (GameplayVisualFactory.Dress), and the colour and sorting order
; (Resources/Art/Readability.json via GameplayReadabilityDefaults). Nothing is left to author.
;
; UNITS: 1 Unity metre = 100 px (World.Ppu), +Y down.
[gd_scene format=3]

[node name="SceneryPiece" type="Sprite2D"]
